{"summary":"Reviewed input/disclosure.md and input/conversation.md (identical text), all eleven draft sections, draft/numerals.md and the four figure briefs. Two workspace conditions limited the review and are stated plainly: (a) figures/ contains only FIG-1.md through FIG-4.md — there are no rendered-*.png images and no review/figure-audit-evidence.json, so no pixel, OCR, leader or endpoint verification was possible and figure review was confined to agreement among each brief, numerals.md and the specification (the absent sheets are already covered by the mechanical "described figure has a drawing sheet" and "numeral appears in a drawing" failures and are not re-reported); (b) prior_art/INDEX.md lists "(none)" and the draft contains no [REF:...] citation, so citation fidelity is vacuous — nothing is characterised that a source could contradict, and the art given to this draft may be incomplete.

On the material that could be checked the draft is faithful. The whole claim chain traces to exact disclosure passages: the four stored per-session values, the allocation-interval subtraction of measured non-charging branch load and configurable safety reserve, minimum-sustaining-first then energy-deficit distribution, pilot send with connector-sensor verification, the verification interval after a reduced pilot command, one-at-a-time shedding in a stored order until branch current is below the limit, the three reclosure conditions, the leakage-threshold welded-contactor record with inhibition of the other contactors and a local fault indicator, offline continuation under the last accepted branch limit, non-start of new sessions without an unexpired signed local token, tamper-evident meter records, and the service-input commissioning map confirmed at the sensing channel. All 27 numerals in numerals.md are used in the detailed description, each labels one part and only that part, and each appears in exactly one figure brief in a consistent role (100/102/104/106/108/118/120/140 on FIG. 1; 108/120/122/124/126/128/130 on FIG. 2; 106/110/112/114/116/132/134 on FIG. 3; 202–216 on FIG. 4). The Brief Description of the Drawings matches what each brief depicts; no figure is said to show anything its brief omits. The abstract is 146 words. The claim-word warning is a false positive: the only "missing" token is the word "claim" from dependent-claim references. All eight defects listed in review/previous-qa.md have been corrected in the current text, including the nonvolatile-memory location for meter records, the bus branch to the connector current sensor in the FIG. 2 brief, the exclusivity assertion about the branch sensor, and the controller/edge-controller lexical link.

Three minor defects remain: one timing inconsistency created by the corrected staged-reduction paragraph, one unsourced function assigned to the local connector identifier, and one unsourced factual assertion about the consequences of an overcurrent-device trip. Each has a local, source-supported fix in the existing text.","findings":[{"severity":"minor","category":"internal_logic","title":"The verification interval is said to start when branch current exceeds the limit, but in the case the same paragraph adds, the reduced pilot command is not sent until the next allocation interval","where":"draft/08-detailed-description.md, paragraph at line 27 (staged reduction step 210)","detail":"The paragraph starts the verification interval and sends the reduced pilot command in one act, then defines the interval as running only after the command has been sent, and then describes a case in which the command is not sent at that moment at all but is the lower assigned limit produced at the next allocation interval. In that case the interval as first stated begins before the command exists, which contradicts the definition in the last sentence and is the gate on which ordered shedding at step 212 and the corresponding limitation of claim 1 ('remaining above the branch current limit for a verification interval after the reduced pilot command has been sent') depend. The three sentences cannot all be true together for the no-identified-channel case. The point is a timing wording slip introduced with the added case rather than an added substantive rule, so I mark it minor; a one-clause change reconciles it and no claim needs to move.","evidence":""When the measured branch current is above the branch current limit, the edge controller 106 sends a reduced pilot command and starts a verification interval. ... Where no connector channel has been so identified, the reduced pilot command is the lower assigned current limit that the available current determination step 202 produces at the next allocation interval from the increased measured non-charging branch load. The verification interval is the period across which the measured branch current must remain above the branch current limit, after the reduced pilot command has been sent, before the ordered shedding step 212 is entered."","fix":"In the paragraph at line 27 replace "the edge controller 106 sends a reduced pilot command and starts a verification interval." with "the edge controller 106 sends a reduced pilot command, and the verification interval begins when that reduced pilot command has been sent." and end the third sentence "...from the increased measured non-charging branch load, and the verification interval begins when that lower assigned current limit is sent.""},{"severity":"minor","category":"disclosure_fidelity","title":"The local connector identifier is described as the bus address by which the controller addresses a connector channel, a function no source gives it","where":"draft/08-detailed-description.md, paragraph at line 9 (and "one addressed connector channel" in the paragraph at line 43)","detail":"The disclosure names the local connector identifier as an element of each connector and gives it exactly two roles: the signed local token "authorizes that connector" by it, and commissioning associates "connector identifiers with physical contactors". The separate sentence about the bus says only "A controller communicates with the connector interfaces and contactors over an isolated local bus." Nothing in the disclosure or the conversation ties the identifier to bus addressing. The candidate supplies that tie and repeats it at commissioning ("one addressed connector channel"). Addressing on a shared bus may be ordinary engineering practice, but common engineering knowledge is not source support for a relationship stated in the specification. No claim depends on it - claims 12 and 13 speak of "a commanded connector channel" - so the added function can be dropped without touching claim support, and the disclosure-supported half of the sentence remains.","evidence":""The first connector channel 120 is associated with a local connector identifier, which is the value by which the edge controller 106 addresses that connector channel over the isolated local bus 108 and under which it stores the state of the charging session at that connector channel."","fix":"In the paragraph at line 9 replace "which is the value by which the edge controller 106 addresses that connector channel over the isolated local bus 108 and under which it stores the state of the charging session at that connector channel" with "which is the value under which the edge controller 106 stores the state of the charging session at that connector channel and which the signed local authorization token names when it authorizes that connector channel". In the paragraph at line 43 replace "one addressed connector channel" with "one commanded connector channel", matching claims 12 and 13."},{"severity":"minor","category":"claim_support","title":"Asserted consequences of a branch overcurrent-device trip are stated as fact in the Background with no source in the workspace","where":"draft/05-background.md, paragraph at line 3, final sentence","detail":"Neither the disclosure nor the conversation says anything about the branch overcurrent device beyond the branch's allowable aggregate current, and prior_art/INDEX.md lists "(none)", so no reference in this workspace supports the stated consequences of a trip, still less the quantified "in many installations". The rest of the Background traces to the inventor's own problem statements (branch limit below the sum of ratings, uncommanded non-charging load, vehicles that do not follow a signalled limit, intermittent connectivity, a mis-recorded wiring map), which is why this one sentence stands out. The equivalent assertion was already removed from the detailed description in the previous review pass; the same characterisation survives here. The point the paragraph is making - that the overcurrent device is the only remaining protection once the control function assumes an allocation has taken effect - is carried by the preceding clause and does not need the trip consequences.","evidence":""Tripping that device removes every charging session on the branch at once, and in many installations it also removes the auxiliary loads sharing the branch."","fix":"Delete the final sentence of the paragraph at line 3 of draft/05-background.md. The preceding sentence, ending "...with the overcurrent device of the branch as the only remaining protection.", is retained unchanged and carries the contrast that the closing paragraph of the Background then draws."}]}
